WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.971
Challenging weather can turn a routine mission into an unsafe or low-value operation.

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:10.488
Wind, gusts, turbulence, moisture, fog, heat, cold, smoke, dust, visibility,

00:00:10.488 --> 00:00:15.456
and rapid change affect the aircraft, crew, sensors, battery, and evidence.

00:00:15.456 --> 00:00:20.424
Plan with current approved information, forecast timing, surface and altitude effects,

00:00:20.424 --> 00:00:23.586
local terrain, precipitation, visibility, temperature, aircraft guidance,

00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:25.392
and mission data requirements.

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:29.909
Define go or no-go criteria before pressure to fly develops.

00:00:29.909 --> 00:00:36.232
At the site, compare forecast with reality, inspect launch and recovery, brief the crew,

00:00:36.232 --> 00:00:38.038
and verify emergency options.

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:43.006
During flight, monitor aircraft behavior, groundspeed, power use, battery, signal, visibility,

00:00:43.006 --> 00:00:44.813
moisture, and image quality.

00:00:44.813 --> 00:00:49.329
Review return-to-home assumptions and preserve manual and emergency landing options.

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:52.943
Abort early when conditions or data quality deteriorate.

00:00:52.943 --> 00:00:57.007
Protect people and property, then document anomalies and lessons.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:01:00.621
The goal is safe, lawful, useful flight data,

00:01:00.621 --> 00:01:06.492
not forcing a mission through conditions that exceed aircraft, crew, or mission limits.

00:01:06.492 --> 00:01:11.460
Delaying or canceling is successful risk management when margin is inadequate.
